caption: Supernatural Unlock Family Catalog
engine-rule: supernatural-unlock-family-catalog

Every mechanically complete Wuxia Tradition defines at least five meaningfully distinct Supernatural Unlock families. Every family must express the Wuxia Tradition's central supernatural theme, but every family is optional during Style construction. A Wuxia Tradition never has a required family and may not make one compulsory indirectly through entry, Duan Wei advancement, or Mastery requirements.

The common family models in Martial Class Details are suggestions and shared player-facing foundations, not mandatory slots. Common models include Conjured Gear, Weapon Mastery, Experience Loop, Embodied Form, Power Talent, and Origin Integration. A Tradition may use, rename, combine where explicitly justified, replace, or omit these models. Conjured Gear and Weapon Mastery are separate models; a Tradition may deliberately combine them only by writing the complete combined family.

Power Talent Family. A single Wuxia Style may define no more than one Power Talent across all of its Supernatural Unlock family implementations. When a family grants Power Talent access, the Style records one legal Power Talent from the catalog designated by the Wuxia Tradition. Use that Power Talent exactly as defined by its governing published GURPS or GATES source; do not separately define or record a Power. Unless a stricter rule applies, the Power Talent follows its normal published rules and is capped at level 4 before the Wuxia Tradition is mastered.

Experience Loop Family. An Experience Loop is a bounded character-point generation method derived from the universal Enhancement Engine. Each Wuxia Tradition that offers Experience Loop as a Supernatural Unlock family must define a closed catalogue of 3-10 approved Experience Loops; at least five is strongly preferred. When a Style selects this family, it selects exactly one approved Experience Loop and records it as part of the Style's institutional instance. Players do not ordinarily design new Experience Loops during Style construction. A GM may approve an additional loop by explicit campaign ruling if it satisfies all requirements, but this is an exception rather than normal Style-construction authority.

For point-generation legality, every approved loop must identify one legal Enhancement Generation Basis and define its qualifying activity and external interaction, exact award rate, spending scope, Enhancement Experience classification and applicable limits, anti-farming controls, and relevant failure or interruption rules. It follows the Enhancement Engine's rules against double counting, consequence-free private repetition, circular cost recovery, self-funding loops, riskless wagering, reversible or inconsequential material investment, and undefined GM-set awards. A Wuxia Experience Loop is not itself a Tier 3 Enhancement Tradition and therefore need not independently provide Enhancement's character-point sharing procedure, independent utility requirement, persistent setting authorship, or Tier 3 Mastery structure unless the individual Wuxia Tradition explicitly adds those features.

Unless the individual Wuxia Tradition explicitly overrides this rule, character points generated by a Wuxia Experience Loop may always be spent on ST, DX, IQ, and HT and on every category Mastery Advantage associated with that Wuxia Tradition’s structural Origin or Origins, in addition to the loop’s locally defined spending scope. These permissions grant no access and waive no prerequisites, caps, purchase timing, or Mastery requirements. An individual Wuxia Tradition may explicitly narrow, delay, replace, or remove this default permission.

Before the practitioner enters the associated Sage Tradition, all awards generated by that Style's Experience Loop have a cumulative lifetime maximum of 500 character points. Reaching this limit stops further character-point generation from that loop until a Sage rule explicitly expands or replaces the cap; it does not erase the loop's fictional consequences or other utility.

Each family definition must state:

- Its legal scope, traits, skills, techniques, forms, equipment, or other purchases.

- Its governing trait and every cap.

- Required Origin, Shido, Pact, Accessibility, and other modifiers.

- Activation, action economy, duration, maintenance, and resource use where relevant.

- Failure, backlash, recovery, and loss where relevant.

- Every access-defining variable that an institutional Style must fix.

- Its interaction with the Domain's Origins and other Traditions.

- If an associated Sage Tradition exists, any family Mastery test and Enhancement that Sage supplies, plus every variable required by the Wuxia Tradition's Advanced Supernatural Unlock procedure.

A Tradition does not complete a family merely by repeating the shared common-family definition. It must add enough Domain- and Tradition-specific rules that a Style can fix every required variable and a player can use the result without a new ruling.

Each Style selects exactly two different families when created. The Style records every required choice before play. The first family opens at Duan Wei 3 and the second at Duan Wei 5. An unlock grants permission to purchase traits; it grants no free character points or free traits.
